GUM_textbook_spacetime	76-105	106-219	Is light actually bent from its straight - line path by the mass of Earth ? How can light , which has no mass , be affected by gravity ?	Einstein preferred to think that it is space and time that are affected by the presence of a large mass ; light beams , and everything else that travels through space and time , then find their paths affected . Light always follows the shortest path — but that path may not always be straight . This idea is true for human travel on the curved surface of planet Earth , as well . Say you want to fly from Chicago to Rome . Since an airplane ca n’t go through the solid body of the Earth , the shortest distance is not a straight line but the arc of a great circle .	Is light actually bent from its straight-line path by the mass of Earth? How can light, which has no mass, be affected by gravity?	Einstein preferred to think that it is space and time that are affected by the presence of a large mass; light beams, and everything else that travels through space and time, then find their paths affected. Light always follows the shortest path—but that path may not always be straight. This idea is true for human travel on the curved surface of planet Earth, as well. Say you want to fly from Chicago to Rome. Since an airplane can’t go through the solid body of the Earth, the shortest distance is not a straight line but the arc of a great circle.	76-105	106-219	Is light actually bent from its straight - line path by the mass of Earth ? How can light , which has no mass , be affected by gravity ?	Einstein preferred to think that it is space and time that are affected by the presence of a large mass ; light beams , and everything else that travels through space and time , then find their paths affected . Light always follows the shortest path — but that path may not always be straight . This idea is true for human travel on the curved surface of planet Earth , as well . Say you want to fly from Chicago to Rome . 